
  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m "Preferences storage that persists across sessions" names a subsystem this repo doesn't
  have — a storage layer, a preferences schema, and a read/write interface other code will depend on. There's no
  existing settings flow to modify, so this isn't a bounded tweak. That means: clarifying questions, 2-3 approaches, a
  sectioned design, then a written spec before any code. Say the word if you think that's heavier than it needs to be.

  First question, and it's the one that determines everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m this repo has two unrelated runtimes, and "session" means a different thing in each. In the browser,
  persistence means [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m — per-device, per-origin, synchronous, no dependencies, and the data never leaves the
  machine. In Node, it means a JSON file on disk (something like [38;5;153m~/.config/drill-test-project/preferences.json[39m) —
  per-machine, async I/O, needs path/permission/corruption handling. A shared abstraction over both is possible but only
  pays off if both surfaces actually need preferences.

  - [1mBrowser only[0m — cheapest, matches the only real UI here (the login form). Rules out nothing permanent; a Node backend
    could be added later against the same schema.
  - [1mNode only[0m — right if the CLI is the real product and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is just fixture noise. Means preferences are invisible
    to the web page.
  - [1mBoth, behind one interface[0m — one [38;5;153mPreferences[39m API with swappable storage backends. Most flexible, roughly double the
    work, and the abstraction is guesswork until I know what each side stores.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser only.[0m The only thing in this repo a user interacts with is the login form, and "settings
  persist across sessions" reads most naturally as that page remembering something. Adding a Node backend later is
  cheap; building the abstraction now is speculative.
